{
  "description": "Trap (audit finding: reverse-charge credit notes above the reverse-charge bills were dropped silently in 3.1(d) and 4(A)(3)). Karnataka client; an unregistered Karnataka advocate firm (legal services, reverse charge marked Yes in the books): bill KD/91 legal fees 20,000 and credit note KD/CN/12 of 50,000 on an August bill (KD/64), both intra-state at 18%. Hand working: RCM bill CGST 1,800 + SGST 1,800; RCM credit note CGST -4,500 + SGST -4,500 -> signed 3.1(d) CGST -2,700, SGST -2,700 (rcmTaxablePaise 20,000 - 50,000 = -30,000; rcmTaxPaise -5,400). Table 3.1(d) cannot be negative, so CGST and SGST are entered as nil and the excess 2,700 + 2,700 = 5,400 is carried forward (rcmCarryForwardPaise 5,400); 4(A)(3) = RCM tax less held back (0) = CGST -2,700 / SGST -2,700 -> entered nil, excess 5,400 carried forward (itcRcmCarryForwardPaise 5,400). Warn gst.negative-rcm lists both amounts and the credit note. Outward 2,00,000 -> CGST 18,000, SGST 18,000 (36,000). 4(A)(5) Sunrise Steel SS/3101 matched to 2B: CGST 9,000 + SGST 9,000 = 18,000; 4(A)(3) nil; available 18,000; 4(B) nil; net 18,000. Set-off (rule 88A): CGST credit 9,000 -> CGST, SGST credit 9,000 -> SGST; reverse-charge cash pos0 = nil (the old code paid nil too: the figures below did not change, only the excess was invisible). Cash CGST 9,000 + SGST 9,000 = 18,000; credit used 18,000; closing credit 0. No expected filing date -> 0 days late, no interest, no late fee. ITC held back 0. The old code had no gst.negative-rcm rule, no carry-forward figures and showed 3.1(d) as CGST -2,700 / SGST -2,700 (a negative entry the return does not accept).",
